Title: "Tuples"
Id: "4b7b092b-b8d4-4bc3-95b0-f3dedaadad85"

Text: "Tuple forms use parentheses and contain two or more elements.

```mlg-fragment
(x_, y_)
(a_, b_, c_)
G ::= (X, *, e)
```

One-element tuples are not supported. Parentheses around a single expression are
grouping syntax, not tuple syntax. An operator may appear as a tuple element, as
`*` does above.

Tuple declarations **destructure**: `G ::= (X, *, e)` introduces `G`, `X`, `*`,
and `e`. Only `::=` introduces these symbols — `:=` requires its right-hand side
to already be in scope.

Destructuring is allowed in a `Defines:`/`Declares:` target, a command
parameter, and a `given:`/`using:` binding:

```mlg-fragment
Declares: M ::= (X, *)
[\magma.element:of{M ::= (X, *)}]
given: M ::= (X, *) is \magma
```

The component types are inferred: from `extends:`/`via` and then `specifies:` for
a `Declares:` target; from the parameter's `when:` type for a command parameter;
and from the right-hand type for a `given:`/`Defines:` binding. Components typed
this way need no separate `when:` entry, and member access reaches them (`M.X`,
`M.*`)."
Id: "6ff385e2-afe6-4d77-b0d3-2a8ba0eceea7"
